Certainly! Here's an example of how you can implement accessible tooltips using semantic HTML, CSS, and ARIA attributes to ensure they're usable for all users, including those relying on assistive technologies:

```html
<div class="tooltip-container">
 <button aria-describedby="tooltip1" class="tooltip-trigger">Hover me</button>
 <div class="tooltip" role="tooltip" id="tooltip1">
 <p>This is an accessible tooltip!</p>
 </div>
</div>
```

```css
.tooltip-container {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
}

.tooltip-trigger {
 margin: 10px;
 padding: 5px 10px;
 font-size: 16px;
 cursor: pointer;
}

.tooltip {
 visibility: hidden;
 background-color: #555;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 4px;
 padding: 5px 10px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 transform: translateX(-50%);
 opacity: 0;
 transition: opacity 0.3s;
}

.tooltip::after {
 content: '';
 position: absolute;
 top: 100%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -5px;
 border-width: 5px;
 border-style: solid;
 border-color: #555 transparent transparent transparent;
}

.tooltip-trigger:hover + .tooltip {
 visibility: visible;
 opacity: 1;
}
```

In this code snippet:
- The `tooltip-trigger` element is a button with an `aria-describedby` attribute that links to the tooltip's ID.
- The `tooltip` element uses the `role="tooltip"` to inform screen readers of its purpose.
- CSS controls the visibility of the tooltip, making it appear on hover by changing its `opacity` and `visibility`.

This implementation ensures that people using screen readers are informed about the presence of the tooltip and can interact with it effectively.

