Absolutely! JavaScript polyfills are essential for supporting features in older browsers that don't natively support newer JavaScript standards. Here's how they fit in:

**JavaScript Polyfills:**
- **Babel-polyfill:** This polyfill can transform modern JavaScript features into a format that older browsers can understand, allowing developers to use the latest syntax while maintaining compatibility.

- **Example of a Specific Polyfill:**
```javascript
if (!String.prototype.padStart) {
 String.prototype.padStart = function padStart(targetLength, padString) {
 targetLength = targetLength >> 0; // Floor if number or convert non-number to 0;
 padString = String(padString || ' ');
 if (this.length > targetLength) {
 return String(this);
 } else {
 targetLength = targetLength - this.length;
 if (targetLength > padString.length) {
 padString += padString.repeat(targetLength / padString.length); // Append to original to ensure we are longer than needed
 }
 return padString.slice(0, targetLength) + String(this);
 }
 };
}
```

By using polyfills, you ensure a consistent user experience across all browsers, especially when dealing with deprecated or unsupported features in older versions.

