```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Tooltip Example</title>
 <style>
 .tooltip-container {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
 cursor: pointer;
 }
 .tooltip {
 visibility: hidden;
 width: 120px;
 background-color: #555;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 6px;
 padding: 5px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -60px;
 opacity: 0;
 transition: opacity 0.3s;
 }
 .tooltip-container:hover .tooltip {
 visibility: visible;
 opacity: 1;
 }
 .tooltip::after {
 content: '';
 position: absolute;
 top: 100%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -5px;
 border-width: 5px;
 border-style: solid;
 border-color: #555 transparent transparent transparent;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <div class="tooltip-container">
 Hover over me
 <span class="tooltip">Tooltip text</span>
 </div>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
- The `.tooltip-container` is the parent element that triggers the visibility of the `.tooltip` when hovered over.
- The `.tooltip` is absolutely positioned and hidden by default. It becomes visible and slightly transparent when the parent container is hovered.
- The `::after` pseudo-element creates an arrow pointing to the tooltip's target, enhancing the visual cue.

This example places the tooltip above the target element. Adjusting the `bottom` and `left` properties can change the tooltip's position.

