Absolutely! Dynamic tooltips can enhance user experience by providing additional information without overwhelming the interface. Here's a simple example of how you can implement an event-driven tooltip using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Interactive Tooltip Example</title>
 <style>
 body {
 font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
 margin: 0;
 padding: 20px;
 }
 .tooltip-container {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
 cursor: pointer;
 }
 .tooltip {
 visibility: hidden;
 background-color: #333;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 5px;
 padding: 5px 10px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 transform: translateX(-50%);
 opacity: 0;
 transition: opacity 0.3s;
 }
 .tooltip::after {
 content: "";
 position: absolute;
 top: 100%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -5px;
 border-width: 5px;
 border-style: solid;
 border-color: #333 transparent transparent transparent;
 }
 .tooltip-visible {
 visibility: visible;
 opacity: 1;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="tooltip-container" id="tooltipContainer">
 Hover over me
 <div class="tooltip" id="tooltip">This is a dynamic tooltip!</div>
</div>

<script>
 const tooltipContainer = document.getElementById('tooltipContainer');
 const tooltip = document.getElementById('tooltip');

 tooltipContainer.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
 tooltip.classList.add('tooltip-visible');
 });

 tooltipContainer.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
 tooltip.classList.remove('tooltip-visible');
 });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- **HTML Structure:** A `div` with the class `tooltip-container` acts as the trigger element. Inside it, another `div` with the class `tooltip` contains the message.
- **CSS Styles:** The tooltip is initially hidden and becomes visible when it is given the `tooltip-visible` class. Transitions create a smooth appearance.
- **JavaScript Interactions:** Event listeners detect mouse entering and leaving the tooltip container, toggling the `tooltip-visible` class to show or hide the tooltip.

