Absolutely! Let's create a simple drag-and-drop interface using HTML5. This example will demonstrate how to handle drop targets and data transfer using the drag-and-drop API.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Drag and Drop Example</title>
 <style>
 .drag-item {
 width: 100px;
 height: 100px;
 background-color: #f0f0f0;
 border: 2px dashed #ccc;
 display: inline-block;
 margin: 10px;
 cursor: move;
 }
 .drop-zone {
 width: 300px;
 height: 300px;
 background-color: #e0e0e0;
 border: 2px dashed #888;
 display: inline-block;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Drag and Drop Interface</h1>
<div id="draggable" class="drag-item" draggable="true">Drag me</div>
<div id="dropzone" class="drop-zone">Drop here</div>

<script>
 const dragItem = document.querySelector('#draggable');
 const dropZone = document.querySelector('#dropzone');

 dragItem.addEventListener('dragstart', (event) => {
 event.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', event.target.id);
 event.target.style.opacity = '0.5';
 });

 dragItem.addEventListener('dragend', (event) => {
 event.target.style.opacity = '1';
 });

 dropZone.addEventListener('dragover', (event) => {
 event.preventDefault();
 });

 dropZone.addEventListener('drop', (event) => {
 event.preventDefault();
 const id = event.dataTransfer.getData('text');
 const draggableElement = document.getElementById(id);
 dropZone.appendChild(draggableElement);
 alert('Item dropped successfully!');
 });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
- A draggable item is created with the `draggable` attribute set to `true`.
- The `dragstart` event is used to store data that will be transferred during the drag operation.
- The `dragover` event on the drop zone allows items to be dropped by preventing the default behavior.
- The `drop` event handles the actual drop operation, moving the dragged item into the drop zone and displaying a success message.

