```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Accessible Drag and Drop</title>
 <style>
 .draggable {
 width: 100px;
 height: 100px;
 margin: 10px;
 background-color: #f9f9f9;
 border: 2px dashed #ccc;
 display: inline-block;
 cursor: move;
 }
 .dropzone {
 width: 300px;
 height: 150px;
 margin: 20px;
 border: 2px solid #bbb;
 background-color: #e9e9e9;
 display: flex;
 justify-content: center;
 align-items: center;
 }
 .draggable:focus {
 outline: 3px solid #5b9dd9;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<div tabindex="0" class="draggable" role="button" aria-grabbed="false" draggable="true">
 <p>Item 1</p>
</div>
<div tabindex="0" class="draggable" role="button" aria-grabbed="false" draggable="true">
 <p>Item 2</p>
</div>

<div class="dropzone" aria-label="Dropzone">
 <p>Drop items here</p>
</div>

<script>
 // JavaScript code to handle drag-and-drop functionality and keyboard navigation will go here.
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Key Features:
- **Semantic HTML** : Using `<div>` elements with appropriate roles and aria attributes to enhance accessibility.
- **Keyboard Navigation** : `tabindex="0"` allows elements to be focusable, which is crucial for keyboard users.
- **Accessible Feedback** : Visual indicators, such as focus outlines, help users understand when an element is selected or active.

This example doesn't include the JavaScript necessary for full functionality, but it lays a solid foundation for accessible drag-and-drop interactions.

