Of course! Here's an example of how you can create a dynamic tooltip using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, with accessibility in mind:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Accessible Tooltip Example</title>
 <style>
 .tooltip-container {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
 cursor: pointer;
 }
 .tooltip-text {
 visibility: hidden;
 background-color: #333;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 4px;
 padding: 5px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -60px;
 width: 120px;
 }
 .tooltip-container:focus .tooltip-text, .tooltip-container:hover .tooltip-text {
 visibility: visible;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <div class="tooltip-container" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="tooltip-desc">
 Hover over me
 <div class="tooltip-text" role="tooltip" id="tooltip-desc">
 Tooltip with more info
 </div>
 </div>

 <script>
 document.querySelectorAll('.tooltip-container').forEach(container => {
 container.addEventListener('focus', () => {
 container.querySelector('.tooltip-text').style.visibility = 'visible';
 });
 container.addEventListener('blur', () => {
 container.querySelector('.tooltip-text').style.visibility = 'hidden';
 });
 });
 </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**
- **HTML**: We use a `div` with `tabindex="0"` to make it focusable for keyboard users. The `aria-describedby` attribute links the tooltip text to the button.
- **CSS**: Styles the tooltip, positioning it and making it visible on hover or focus.
- **JavaScript**: Listens for focus and blur events to toggle tooltip visibility, ensuring that keyboard users can also access the tooltip.

