Of course! Tooltips are a great way to provide additional information without cluttering your UI. It's crucial to ensure they're accessible to all users. Here's a simple example of an accessible tooltip using HTML and CSS:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Accessible Tooltip Example</title>
 <style>
 .tooltip {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
 border-bottom: 1px dotted black; /* Indicator that there's more info */
 }

 .tooltip .tooltiptext {
 visibility: hidden;
 width: 200px;
 background-color: #333;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 5px;
 padding: 10px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -100px;

 /* Accessibility */
 opacity: 0;
 transition: opacity 0.3s;
 }

 .tooltip:focus .tooltiptext, .tooltip:hover .tooltiptext {
 visibility: visible;
 opacity: 1;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="tooltip" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="tooltip-text">
 Hover over me
 <div class="tooltiptext" id="tooltip-text">Tooltip text explaining more details</div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**

- The `.tooltip` class wraps the element that will trigger the tooltip. It uses `position: relative` to position the tooltip text.
- The `.tooltiptext` class contains the actual tooltip text and appears above the trigger element when hovered or focused.
- **Accessibility Features:**
 - `visibility: hidden; opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.3s;` ensures a smooth fade-in effect when the tooltip becomes visible.
 - `tabindex="0"` makes the tooltip focusable, allowing keyboard users to access it.
 - `aria-describedby="tooltip-text"` provides screen readers with a description of the element's purpose.
- Ensure sufficient color contrast for readability, here using a dark background with white text.

