Certainly! Tooltips are a great way to offer additional information to users without cluttering the interface. Here's a basic example of how to create a tooltip component in React and style it using CSS-in-JS:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import styled from '@emotion/styled';

// CSS-in-JS styling for the tooltip
const TooltipContainer = styled.div`
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
`;

const TooltipText = styled.div`
 visibility: ${(props) => (props.visible ? 'visible' : 'hidden')};
 width: 120px;
 background-color: black;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 5px;
 padding: 5px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -60px;
 opacity: ${(props) => (props.visible ? 1 : 0)};
 transition: opacity 0.3s;

 &:after {
 content: '';
 position: absolute;
 top: 100%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -5px;
 border-width: 5px;
 border-style: solid;
 border-color: black transparent transparent transparent;
 }
`;

const Tooltip = ({ text, children }) => {
 const [visible, setVisible] = useState(false);

 return (
 <TooltipContainer
 onMouseEnter={() => setVisible(true)}
 onMouseLeave={() => setVisible(false)}
 >
 {children}
 <TooltipText visible={visible}>{text}</TooltipText>
 </TooltipContainer>
 );
};

export default Tooltip;
```

### Explanation:
1. **TooltipContainer:** This wraps the element that will have a tooltip. It's styled to be inline-block to allow position control.
2. **TooltipText:** This is the actual tooltip text, positioned absolutely relative to the container. It shows when `visible` is `true`.
3. **Visibility Control:** We toggle the `visible` state on mouse enter and leave using `setVisible`.

### Usage:
```jsx
<Tooltip text="This is a tooltip">
 <button>Hover me</button>
</Tooltip>
```

In this setup, hovering over the button displays the tooltip with the message "This is a tooltip". Adjust the styles as needed to fit the design of your application.

