Of course! Creating a tooltip that is accessible and responsive is crucial for ensuring all users have a great experience on your site. Below is an example of a simple tooltip component with considerations for accessibility and responsive design:

```html
<div class="tooltip-container">
 <button class="tooltip-trigger" aria-describedby="tooltip-1">
 Hover over me
 </button>
 <div class="tooltip" id="tooltip-1" role="tooltip">
 <p>This is an accessible tooltip.</p>
 </div>
</div>

<style>
 .tooltip-container {
 position: relative;
 display: inline-block;
 }

 .tooltip {
 visibility: hidden;
 background-color: #333;
 color: #fff;
 text-align: center;
 border-radius: 4px;
 padding: 8px;
 position: absolute;
 z-index: 1;
 bottom: 125%;
 left: 50%;
 margin-left: -60px;
 opacity: 0;
 transition: opacity 0.3s;
 width: 120px;
 }

 .tooltip-trigger:hover + .tooltip,
 .tooltip-trigger:focus + .tooltip {
 visibility: visible;
 opacity: 1;
 }

 @media (max-width: 600px) {
 .tooltip {
 width: 100px;
 bottom: 150%;
 margin-left: -50px;
 }
 }
</style>
```

**Key Features:**
- **Accessibility:** The tooltip uses `aria-describedby` to associate the button with the tooltip, ensuring screen readers announce the tooltip content.
- **Responsive Design:** The width of the tooltip adjusts for smaller screens using media queries.

Feel free to modify the design and functionality to better suit your needs!

