Of course! Here's an example of a simple drag-and-drop interface implemented using HTML and CSS. This example demonstrates how items can be dragged and dropped between two lists.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Drag and Drop Example</title>
 <style>
 body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; }
 .container { display: flex; justify-content: space-around; margin-top: 50px; }
 .list {
 border: 2px dashed #ccc;
 padding: 20px;
 width: 200px;
 min-height: 200px;
 background-color: #f9f9f9;
 transition: background-color 0.3s;
 }
 .list.drag-over {
 background-color: #e3f2fd;
 }
 .item {
 padding: 10px;
 margin: 5px;
 background-color: #90caf9;
 cursor: grab;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>
 <div class="container">
 <div class="list" id="list1">
 <div class="item" draggable="true">Item 1</div>
 <div class="item" draggable="true">Item 2</div>
 </div>
 <div class="list" id="list2">
 <div class="item" draggable="true">Item 3</div>
 <div class="item" draggable="true">Item 4</div>
 </div>
 </div>

 <script>
 const lists = document.querySelectorAll('.list');
 let draggedItem = null;

 lists.forEach(list => {
 list.addEventListener('dragstart', e => {
 draggedItem = e.target;
 setTimeout(() => e.target.style.display = 'none', 0);
 });

 list.addEventListener('dragend', e => {
 setTimeout(() => {
 e.target.style.display = 'block';
 draggedItem = null;
 }, 0);
 });

 list.addEventListener('dragover', e => {
 e.preventDefault();
 });

 list.addEventListener('dragenter', e => {
 e.preventDefault();
 list.classList.add('drag-over');
 });

 list.addEventListener('dragleave', () => {
 list.classList.remove('drag-over');
 });

 list.addEventListener('drop', () => {
 list.appendChild(draggedItem);
 list.classList.remove('drag-over');
 });
 });
 </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates two areas where items can be dragged between them. The visual feedback is enhanced with CSS by changing the background color when an item is dragged over a list. The script uses events like `dragstart`, `dragover`, `dragend`, and `drop` to manage the drag-and-drop behavior.

