Certainly! Here's an example of a modal window using HTML5 and CSS3. This modal can be closed by clicking on a close button. It's also designed to be mobile-responsive:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Responsive Modal Example</title>
 <style>
 body {
 font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
 margin: 0;
 padding: 0;
 }
 .modal {
 display: none; /* Hidden by default */
 position: fixed;
 z-index: 1;
 left: 0;
 top: 0;
 width: 100%;
 height: 100%;
 overflow: auto;
 background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.4); /* Black w/ opacity */
 }
 .modal-content {
 background-color: #fefefe;
 margin: 15% auto;
 padding: 20px;
 border: 1px solid #888;
 width: 80%; /* Responsive width */
 max-width: 500px;
 }
 .close {
 color: #aaa;
 float: right;
 font-size: 28px;
 font-weight: bold;
 }
 .close:hover,
 .close:focus {
 color: black;
 text-decoration: none;
 cursor: pointer;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>Responsive Modal Example</h2>

<!-- Trigger/Open The Modal -->
<button id="myBtn">Open Modal</button>

<!-- The Modal -->
<div id="myModal" class="modal">
 <!-- Modal content -->
 <div class="modal-content">
 <span class="close">&times;</span>
 <p>This is a simple modal window. You can include any content here.</p>
 </div>
</div>

<script>
 // Get the modal
 var modal = document.getElementById("myModal");

 // Get the button that opens the modal
 var btn = document.getElementById("myBtn");

 // Get the <span> element that closes the modal
 var span = document.getElementsByClassName("close")[0];

 // When the user clicks the button, open the modal 
 btn.onclick = function() {
 modal.style.display = "block";
 }

 // When the user clicks on <span> (x), close the modal
 span.onclick = function() {
 modal.style.display = "none";
 }

 // When the user clicks anywhere outside of the modal, close it
 window.onclick = function(event) {
 if (event.target == modal) {
 modal.style.display = "none";
 }
 }
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**How It Works:**
- The modal is initially hidden using `display: none;`.
- Clicking the button with the ID `myBtn` changes the modal's `display` property to `block`, making it visible.
- The modal can be closed by clicking the "x" symbol or clicking outside of the modal.
- The CSS ensures the modal is centered and scales appropriately on different screen sizes.

